TTo calculate the required fuel mass, tank volume, and fuel consumption rate for a 20 kg turbojet UAV, you need to work backward from the engine's thrust requirement and its Thrust Specific Fuel Consumption (TSFC).
Step 1: Determine Thrust Required for Cruise
For a 20 kg aircraft in steady, level cruise, the engine does not need to produce 20 kg of thrust (approx. 196 N). Instead, thrust must equal aerodynamic drag:

$$\text{Thrust Required } (T) = \frac{\text{Total Weight}}{\text{Lift-to-Drag Ratio } (L/D)}$$
Typical Turbojet UAV $L/D$: ~6 to 10.

Calculation (assuming $L/D = 8$):

$$T = \frac{20\text{ kg}}{8} = 2.5\text{ kg of thrust} \approx 24.5\text{ N}$$
Accounting for climb, maneuvering, and acceleration margins, average cruise thrust typically operates at roughly 30 N to 40 N (~3 to 4 kgf).

Step 2: Calculate Mass Fuel Consumption Rate
Small micro-turbojets (e.g., JetCat P100, KingTech K85) have a TSFC of roughly 0.12 to 0.15 kg / (N · hr) at cruise throttle settings.

Using a conservative TSFC of $0.14\text{ kg/(N}\cdot\text{hr)}$ at an average cruise thrust of $35\text{ N}$:

$$\text{Fuel Flow Rate } (\dot{m}) = T \times \text{TSFC}$$
$$\dot{m} = 35\text{ N} \times 0.14\text{ kg/(N}\cdot\text{hr)} = 4.9\text{ kg/hr}$$
Converting this to a per-minute rate:

$$\text{Per-minute consumption} = \frac{4.9\text{ kg/hr}}{60} \approx \mathbf{0.0817\text{ kg/min}} \quad (\approx 81.7\text{ g/min})$$
Step 3: Calculate Required Usable Fuel Mass
For a 15-minute primary flight mission:

$$\text{Mission Fuel Mass} = 15\text{ min} \times 0.0817\text{ kg/min} = \mathbf{1.225\text{ kg}}$$
Adding a standard 20% fuel reserve (for taxi, climb, wind headroom, and landing setup):

$$\text{Total Fuel Mass Required} = 1.225\text{ kg} \times 1.20 = \mathbf{1.47\text{ kg}}$$
Step 4: Convert Mass to Tank Volume
Jet-A / Kerosene has a density of approximately $0.80\text{ kg/L}$ at standard temperature ($15^\circ\text{C}$).

$$\text{Total Tank Volume Required} = \frac{\text{Total Fuel Mass}}{\text{Fuel Density}}$$
$$\text{Total Tank Volume Required} = \frac{1.47\text{ kg}}{0.80\text{ kg/L}} = \mathbf{1.8375\text{ Liters}} \approx \mathbf{1,840\text{ ml}}$$
