System Sizing Recommendation
To achieve this 1850 ml total capacity safely on your 20 kg frame:

Main Tank: Install a 1,500 ml composite saddle tank located directly over the Center of Gravity.

Hopper Tank (UAT): Install a 350 ml Ultimate Air Trap tank downstream of the main tank to feed bubble-free fuel directly to the high-pressure pump

Because turbojet UAVs operate at significantly higher speeds than propeller-driven drones, parasite drag (skin friction and form drag) plays a much larger role than induced drag

.To maximize the Lift-to-Drag ($L/D$) ratio and fuel efficiency of a 20 kg turbojet UAV, you must optimize three primary areas: airfoil selection (wing profile), wing planform geometry, and cruise speed matching.

1. Wing Profile (Airfoil) Selection & Optimization
Unlike high-lift, highly-cambered airfoils used on slow surveillance drones, a turbojet UAV requires a low-drag, medium-thin profile designed for higher Reynolds numbers.

Thickness-to-Chord Ratio ($t/c$): Aim for a thickness of 10% to 12%. Thinner sections reduce form drag and delay compressibility effects at high speeds, while still leaving enough internal height for a structural spar.

Camber Selection: Choose a low-to-moderate camber. High camber creates excessive pitching moments and drag at high speeds. Profiles like the MH-32, E1210, or specialized laminar-flow supersonic/transonic sections help maintain extended laminar boundary layers, reducing skin friction drag.

Surface Finish: Maintain an extremely smooth, glossy finish on the composite wing skin. Surface roughness trips the boundary layer into turbulence prematurely, sharply increasing skin friction drag.

2. Planform and Geometry Optimization
How the wing is shaped in plan view dictates how efficiently it generates lift relative to its span.

Aspect Ratio ($AR$): Induced drag is inversely proportional to aspect ratio ($AR = b^2/S$). While ultra-high aspect ratios (like gliders) cause structural weight penalties on a heavy jet, maintaining an $AR$ between 7 and 9 provides an optimal sweet spot between low induced drag and structural rigidity.

Taper Ratio and Twist (Washout): Use a taper ratio of roughly 0.5 to 0.6 (tip chord shorter than root chord) to approximate an elliptical lift distribution, which naturally minimizes induced drag. Add 1 to 2 degrees of geometric washout (twist) at the wingtips to prevent tip stall during high-G maneuvers.

Winglets / Tip Fences: Adding clean, blended winglets or raked wingtips reduces wingtip vortex drag, effectively increasing the aerodynamic aspect ratio without adding massive wingspan.

3. Cruise Speed and Operating Point Tuning
For a turbojet, fuel consumption is directly proportional to thrust produced. To maximize efficiency, you must fly at the exact airspeed where aerodynamic efficiency ($L/D$) is maximized.

Fly at Maximum $L/D$ Speed ($V_{md}$): Maximum range for a jet aircraft occurs precisely at the angle of attack and airspeed that yields the maximum $L/D$ ratio (where parasite drag equals induced drag). You can calculate this target cruise velocity using:

$$V_{cruise} = \sqrt{\frac{2 \cdot W}{\rho \cdot S \cdot C_{L\text{\_at\_max\_L/D}}}}$$
(Where $W$ is aircraft weight, $\rho$ is air density, $S$ is wing area, and $C_L$ is the lift coefficient corresponding to the peak of the $L/D$ curve).

Avoid High-Speed Drag Spikes: Turbojet fuel burn skyrockets if pushed past its optimal cruise envelope into transonic drag divergence zones. Keep cruise speeds safely below the critical Mach number ($M_{crit}$) of your chosen airfoil.

